== 15. He Who Neglects the Closet Is Neglected in Public

He who willingly neglects private prayer will certainly be neglected in his public prayer. He who will not call on God in secret will find by sad experience that God will neither hear him nor regard him in public. The lack of private duties is the great reason why the hearts of many are so dead and dull, so formal and carnal, so barren and unfruitful under public ordinances. Oh, that Christians would seriously lay this to heart! Certainly, that man’s or woman’s heart is best in public who is most frequent in private. Those who are most conscientiously exercised in closet duties make the most gains in public ordinances. No man’s graces rise so high, nor do any man’s experiences rise so high, nor does any man’s communion with God rise so high, nor do any man’s divine enjoyments rise so high, nor do any man’s springs of comfort rise so high, nor do any man’s hopes rise so high, nor do any man’s talents and gifts rise so high, as theirs do who conscientiously wait on God in their closets before they wait on Him in the assembly of His people, and who, when they return from public ordinances, retire into their closets and look up to heaven for a blessing on the public means. It is certain that private duties fit the soul for public ordinances. He who makes it a matter of conscience to wait on God in private will find by experience that God will wonderfully bless public ordinances to him (Mic 2:7). My design is not to set up one ordinance of God above another, nor to make one ordinance of God clash with another—the public with the private, or the private with the public—but that every ordinance may have its proper place and right, the desire of my soul being to prize every ordinance, and to praise every ordinance, and to practice every ordinance, and to make use of every ordinance, and to bless the Lord for every ordinance. But if you#super[+] would ever see the beauty and glory of God in His sanctuary, if you#super[+] would ever have public ordinances be lovely and lively to your#super[+] souls, if you#super[+] would ever have your#super[+] drooping spirits revived, and your#super[+] languishing souls refreshed, and your#super[+] weak graces strengthened, and your#super[+] strong corruptions weakened under public ordinances, be more careful and conscientious in the performance of closet duties (Ps 63:1–3). Oh, how strong in grace! Oh, how victorious over sin! Oh, how dead to the world! Oh, how alive to Christ! Oh, how fit to live! Oh, how prepared to die many a Christian might have been, had they only been more frequent, serious, and conscientious in the discharge of closet duties! And yet I do think there is a truth in that saying of Bede—the word #emph[church] being rightly understood—that he who does not come willingly to church will one day go unwillingly to hell.
